<TopicPage slug="subjects/artificial-intelligence/expert/robotics-and-embodied-ai">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Embodied AI puts learning systems <strong>into the physical world, where mistakes break
    things</strong>. Robotics combines perception, planning and control under real-time
    constraints — and unlike chatbots, a robot's errors have mass and momentum. The recent
    shift: learning-based approaches (trained in simulation and from demonstration) replacing
    hand-programmed motion, moving the field toward general-purpose machines.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the embodied loop, its map-making, and the bridges from simulation" widget="equationStep" config={roboCfg} />

  <h2>Where robots already work — and why</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Structured environments first:</strong> warehouses (a million Amazon robots), factories, surgical systems. Predictable settings, high value per movement, constrained tasks — the economics favour them.</li>
    <li><strong>The unstructured world is harder:</strong> homes are chaos — every object different, every task novel. General-purpose household robots remain the field's white whale; foundation models are the newest attempt at the whales' migration route.</li>
    <li><strong>Autonomous vehicles as a case study in over-promising:</strong> "fully self-driving by 2018" became "assistance features with constant supervision". Robotics timelines are humbling — the last 1% of edge cases takes 99% of the time.</li>
    <li><strong>Safety is embodied:</strong> a chatbot error costs a paragraph; a robot error costs a limb. Verification, hardware limits and graceful degradation are design requirements, not features.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cadena et al., "Past, Present, and Future of SLAM" (2016); OpenAI Rubik's Cube hand (2019); DeepMind RT-2 (2023); Boston Dynamics video archive.</p>
</TopicPage>
